WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.783
Baldur's Gate 3 is a game filled with choices.

00:00:02.783 --> 00:00:04.850
Choices that have consequences.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:08.483
Just like what it's based off of: Dungeons and Dragons

00:00:12.566 --> 00:00:15.500
At the heart of any unforgettable Dungeons
and Dragons campaign

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.750
is a great Dungeon Master.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.266
It's their responsibility
to guide you on a grand adventure

00:00:20.266 --> 00:00:22.966
and make sure the world reacts
to your actions

00:00:22.966 --> 00:00:25.100
in a way that feels natural and unplanned.

00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:29.683
It's collaborative storytelling between the players,
the world and the DM, orchestrating

00:00:29.683 --> 00:00:34.183
it all so every action, choice and consequence
feels like it really matters.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:38.233
Now, that's tabletop D&amp;D,
but that sense of freedom and roleplaying

00:00:38.266 --> 00:00:40.966
isn't that far removed from its video game
counterpart...

00:00:41.566 --> 00:00:42.883
Baldur's Gate.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.483
The Baldur's Gate franchise is built
on capturing the tabletop

00:00:46.483 --> 00:00:49.583
play style, and Baldur's Game
3 is no exception.

00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:53.683
Operating on D&amp;D's
latest fifth edition ruleset.

00:00:54.866 --> 00:00:58.500
For developer Larian Studios to
truly that D&amp;D essence

00:00:58.516 --> 00:00:59.716
It has to evoke that

00:00:59.716 --> 00:01:03.716
collaborative storytelling relationship
between the player and the game.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.016
But somewhere early
in the making of Baldur's Gate 3

00:01:06.016 --> 00:01:10.133
the game's dungeon
master made a very small decision

00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:14.233
that sent its development
into a cascading spiral of problems

00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:18.916
that has been a big old challenge
to solve, and all it involved was getting you

00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:23.566
the player
to acquire one very specific item.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:27.416
SWEN: This is a story about a literally insane
amount of versions of the story

00:01:27.416 --> 00:01:28.966
of how you're going
to get this bloody box.

00:01:28.966 --> 00:01:34.516
That is Swen, he's the Dungeon Master,
or director, rather, of Baldur’s Gate 3.

00:01:34.766 --> 00:01:36.350
Since he's not sitting across from you

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:40.033
at a kitchen table for every role
of your dice, Swen and his team

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:44.566
instead had intricately design the game
to account for every possible outcome,

00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:48.016
every possible choice you might make.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.266
I try to figure out for players
interesting things to do,

00:01:51.266 --> 00:01:52.400
and then I try to predict

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.350
how they're going to be doing them
as if they figured out the solution

00:01:55.783 --> 00:01:59.266
but I've prepared everything for them so
that when they figure out their solution,

00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:02.516
they get a reward
that is better than what they expected.

00:02:02.666 --> 00:02:07.966
These solutions and rewards that Swen has
created, are referred to as permutations,

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.966
the possible variations of a choice

00:02:10.966 --> 00:02:16.250
you make, the branching outcomes of chance
and possibility with every choice

00:02:16.466 --> 00:02:22.466
leading to other choices, most of which
are determined by the role of a dice

00:02:24.316 --> 00:02:27.533
A small example would be touch the brain,
don't touch the brain,

00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:31.566
pull the brain out, try to kill the brain,
fail at killing the brain.

00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:34.333
Now you have to fight the brain.

00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:36.466
To larger repercussions that can have

00:02:36.466 --> 00:02:40.016
a ripple effect down the line,
like choosing to help someone, betray them,

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:40.850
Kill them.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:42.133
Let them join your party.

00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:44.000
And so on. And so forth.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.100
The choices
you make are completely up to you

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.950
because a good Dungeon Master
never says no.

00:02:49.133 --> 00:02:54.683
It takes your choices and runs with it,
except in very, very rare occurrences.

00:02:54.683 --> 00:02:59.183
Like when Swen decided
you needed to have this box.

00:02:59.183 --> 00:03:00.733
Well, sort of a box.

00:03:00.733 --> 00:03:03.133
[SWEN] I keep on calling it the box,
but it really is an artifact.

00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:05.033
But it used to be a box.
[KURT] In the game.

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:06.166
It's called the Mysterious Artifact.

00:03:06.166 --> 00:03:08.033
But yeah, it's a box.

00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:10.066
It belongs to a character named Shadowheart.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.766
The box might be the key
to the overall story,

00:03:12.766 --> 00:03:17.216
but it's also tied up in her story to.
[Shadowheart] The artifact is being hunted.

00:03:17.216 --> 00:03:19.100
And so I'm being hunted.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:23.600
[KURT] And so one way or another, you're going
to need to get the box from Shadowheart.

00:03:23.633 --> 00:03:26.750
[SWEN] We very rarely
have things that you must experience,

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.500
but it's really important that you get this artifact.
 [KURT] For Swen and his team,

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:34.283
The objective became getting the player
to receive this item as naturally

00:03:34.283 --> 00:03:37.850
and as elegantly as possible without ever
forcing the player to take it.

00:03:38.083 --> 00:03:42.566
It had to feel like it was the player's
choice in order to maintain this idea

00:03:42.866 --> 00:03:44.000
of player freedom.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.250
At first,
the possibilities to have this box

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.883
come your way
seem pretty straightforward. An easy

00:03:48.883 --> 00:03:52.616
Solution would be to just position
Shadowheart in several places

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:53.750
that the player

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:57.466
could potentially run into her.
One point the tutorial area of the Nautiloid

00:03:57.466 --> 00:04:00.916
and if not there, then there's a chance
to cross paths with her on the beach.

00:04:00.916 --> 00:04:04.850
And if not there, then there's a chance
to cross paths with her in Druid’s Grove.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:06.350
and so on and so on.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.366
[Shadowheart] I was starting to think
I was the only survivor.

00:04:08.383 --> 00:04:11.733
[KURT] It started
with very basic permutations.

00:04:11.783 --> 00:04:13.033
A: You invite her to your party.

00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:14.300
Great. Problem solved.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.133
You have the box.

00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:17.366
It is in your party
[Shadowheart] Right then.

00:04:17.366 --> 00:04:19.850
Lead on. 
[KURT] B: You can kill her.

00:04:22.483 --> 00:04:23.266
Take the box.

00:04:23.266 --> 00:04:24.316
Morally wishy washy.

00:04:24.316 --> 00:04:26.716
But hey,
you still got the box, way to go.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.716
But what if you don't meet her?

00:04:30.733 --> 00:04:33.583
What if you walk past her,
you don't talk to her.

00:04:33.583 --> 00:04:35.983
And if you do,
you don't invite her to your party.

00:04:36.083 --> 00:04:37.283
What if you choose to jump off

00:04:37.283 --> 00:04:40.100
a ledge and injure yourself
rather than cross paths with her?

00:04:42.266 --> 00:04:45.133
What if you just don't like her
and want nothing to do with her?

00:04:45.216 --> 00:04:45.833
Yeah.

00:04:46.216 --> 00:04:48.766
So they started working on ways
to entice the player.

00:04:48.766 --> 00:04:51.016
Like making it very obvious

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:54.766
Shadowheart has an item of importance
while shrouding it in mystery.

00:04:54.833 --> 00:04:55.966
[Shadowheart] There's no story.

00:04:55.966 --> 00:04:58.433
None that you're entitled to here anyway.

00:04:58.433 --> 00:05:00.083
Just forget you ever saw it.

00:05:00.083 --> 00:05:02.900
[KURT] But what if that wasn't enough?

00:05:05.033 --> 00:05:06.316
Here's one way to think about it.

00:05:06.316 --> 00:05:09.766
Imagine playing
the legend of Zelda Ocarina of Time

00:05:09.766 --> 00:05:12.116
and just never picking up the ocarina

00:05:14.083 --> 00:05:16.900
or just avoid meeting Zelda altogether.

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:21.500
And yet the game continues on. The box
in Baldur’s

00:05:21.533 --> 00:05:27.216
Gate 3 is what the ocarina
and Zelda are to Ocarina of Time.

00:05:27.233 --> 00:05:30.650
The simple task of enticing the player
with not only the box, but the character

00:05:30.666 --> 00:05:34.483
Shadowheart
started to completely unravel.

00:05:34.583 --> 00:05:39.083
It led to what Swenrefers
to as “permutation insanity”

00:05:39.383 --> 00:05:42.733
Our entire design
is built around your ability to recover

00:05:42.733 --> 00:05:47.066
from a catastrophe by doing things
that the game allows you to do.

00:05:47.216 --> 00:05:49.516
And the box is a good example of that
because

00:05:49.516 --> 00:05:51.316
it involves just a couple of mechanics.

00:05:51.316 --> 00:05:52.550
You can kill her.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:55.366
You can steal from her,
and you can move the object.

00:05:55.616 --> 00:05:58.766
These three things
in the hands of a devious player

00:05:59.116 --> 00:06:01.400
caused a tremendous 
amount of permutations

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.483
that we need to cover.
[KURT] Between the tutorial and the opening area

00:06:04.516 --> 00:06:09.916
Permutations to meet Shadowheart and
receive the box are in the dozens alone.

00:06:10.166 --> 00:06:12.716
It only gets more complicated
as the story goes forward.

00:06:12.833 --> 00:06:17.666
All of these possibilities
need to be written, voice acted, animated,

00:06:17.933 --> 00:06:22.016
and scripted. Scenarios
that included Shadowheart or didn’t.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:24.533
[SWEN] There's somebody who has to write
all of this dialog.

00:06:24.566 --> 00:06:25.583
His name is John,

00:06:25.583 --> 00:06:27.833
and he just keeps on asking questions
about the box and well,

00:06:27.866 --> 00:06:29.400
what in this case and what in this case?

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.050
And the cases just kept on adding and
adding and adding and adding and adding.

00:06:33.233 --> 00:06:35.600
I was like,
Oh my God, I don’t know. It comes from the sky.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.750
It falls on your head.
I don't know anymore.

00:06:37.766 --> 00:06:39.533
I don't know anymore,
but it's too late now.

00:06:39.533 --> 00:06:40.883
You need to get the box and Shadowheart

00:06:40.883 --> 00:06:42.016
she cannot live without the box.

00:06:42.016 --> 00:06:44.066
So the box has to be there.

00:06:44.066 --> 00:06:44.633
There's now

00:06:44.633 --> 00:06:48.566
hundreds of permutations in the game
with some more elegant than others.

00:06:48.683 --> 00:06:49.916
You may find yourself playing

00:06:49.916 --> 00:06:53.366
BG3 in very rare permutations
where suddenly you can fall on your knees

00:06:53.366 --> 00:06:55.400
and you're going to need that box
and Shadowheart is going to pop up 

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.833
I'm here with the box in my hand.

00:06:58.033 --> 00:07:00.650
Use this box to protect you, and you say,
Who the hell are you?

00:07:00.766 --> 00:07:02.783
I followed you and I saw you.

00:07:02.983 --> 00:07:04.250
So it's really ridiculous dialog

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:06.466
that really doesn't make that much sense,
but it works.

00:07:06.466 --> 00:07:09.566
I mean, somehow
and you it's a very confusing moment,

00:07:09.983 --> 00:07:12.583
so it might still change, but at this
moment that's the best that we have.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:13.483
[Shadowheart] You were lucky.

00:07:13.483 --> 00:07:15.766
I managed to catch up with you when I did.

00:07:15.766 --> 00:07:17.383
We're both lucky, in fact.

00:07:17.383 --> 00:07:20.083
[KURT] All Things Considered, Swen never went against

00:07:20.083 --> 00:07:23.366
the principles
of a good Dungeon Master. In his design,

00:07:23.366 --> 00:07:27.200
He never wanted to say no to the player -
to give you all this freedom

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.950
and then snatch it away with a bottleneck
that forces the item onto you.

00:07:31.283 --> 00:07:32.516
Like a good DM

00:07:32.516 --> 00:07:34.916
he's not railroading
you down a specific path,

00:07:35.116 --> 00:07:38.966
but that isn't without a great deal
of hardship in having integrity

00:07:38.966 --> 00:07:40.283
in the game's design.

00:07:40.283 --> 00:07:43.433
[KURT] I feel shame. I guess I couldn't
come up with something better.

00:07:43.766 --> 00:07:45.833
I mean, like it
because this one is really my doing.

00:07:45.833 --> 00:07:46.466
It's not a team.

00:07:46.466 --> 00:07:47.716
Like I was the one that the idiot

00:07:47.716 --> 00:07:49.750
that came up with this thing
with the box doing it

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:51.283
and I'm giving it to Shadowheart.

00:07:51.283 --> 00:07:54.600
I know my intentions were noble,
but my, my, my design was flawed.

00:07:55.833 --> 00:07:58.950
This is permutation insanity. 
[KURT] As of this video,

00:07:59.116 --> 00:08:04.316
Baldur’s Gate 3 is still in development
but available to play in early access,

00:08:04.316 --> 00:08:09.116
which means the box problem
isn't completely solved yet.

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:13.400
[KURT] How many actual man hours do
you think have gone into this?
[SWEN] In the box?

00:08:13.766 --> 00:08:15.016
I don't want to know.

00:08:15.016 --> 00:08:16.316
I don't want to know how much man hours

00:08:16.316 --> 00:08:19.333
went into it because the pipeline
that's behind these things is really big.

00:08:19.583 --> 00:08:21.500
It's the scripting and the writing.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:22.816
But then there's a voice recording,

00:08:22.816 --> 00:08:25.933
there's a cinematic design,
the lighting, the VFX, the SFX,

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:28.850
everything that goes with its cinematic
animation, motion capture, cleaning.

00:08:28.966 --> 00:08:30.416
I think if they're going to add up
everything it's

00:08:30.416 --> 00:08:32.933
going to be quite a few man-months,
if not man-year,

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:36.133
just because of flawed design.

00:08:36.133 --> 00:08:38.116
[KURT] Some games are set paths.

00:08:38.116 --> 00:08:41.333
Stories meant to be experienced
as the creator had intended.

00:08:41.666 --> 00:08:44.816
All other games
invite you to craft the story with it,

00:08:45.083 --> 00:08:49.850
but in doing so, a lot of responsibility
is put on the makers to make sure you feel

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:52.100
that sense of agency in a story

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:56.800
that really has already been meticulously
laid out for you.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:08:59.966
Or you
can just Shadowheart in the opening

00:08:59.966 --> 00:09:02.700
15 minutes of the game, see the box
eventually invite you to your party

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:06.316
and just never wonder for a second
that there was any other possibility,

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:10.433
let alone hundreds.
[SWEN] It was a very small thing

00:09:10.433 --> 00:09:12.616
that we just wanted to give you the box

